docs/architecture.md
     1	# Architecture (Python reference implementation)
     2	
     3	Centralised PC-side brain (legal per reglamento s6) consuming official telemetry v1 and emitting per-wheel speed
     4	commands (mm/s) to each rover. Embedded code later only needs: command receiver + wheel speed loop + watchdog.
     5	
     6	```
     7	TCP NDJSON (vision / sim emulator)
     8	   -> vision/parser.py      official cells/row-down/deg -> Frame (mm, y-up, rad, capture times)
     9	   -> coordination/supervisor.py
    10	        estimation/pose_estimator.py   EKF per rover [x,y,theta,v,omega], delayed-measurement replay, gating, GOOD/DEGRADED/LOST
    11	        estimation/cube_tracker.py     per cube position filter, stale-growth, unexpected-move detection, orientation belief
    12	        planning/push_planner.py       delivery candidates (eroded depot), multi-leg push plans, feasibility, ranking
    13	        planning/task_allocator.py     exhaustive assignment x order x plan choice, makespan objective, both-rovers rule
    14	        planning/navigation.py         lattice/hybrid A* over (x,y,theta) with oriented footprint, rotate/straight primitives
    15	        planning/reservations.py       spatial corridor reservations (not time-indexed), priorities, imminent-collision check
    16	        rover/fsm.py                   RoverAgent FSM per rover (states, timeouts, failure paths)
    17	        control/controllers.py         segment follower, align, capture, push (heading + cross-track PD), retreat
    18	   -> WheelCommand per rover (and later ESP-NOW status: coordination/protocol.py)
    19	simulation/  physics (quasi-static pushing, U-shaped rover), sensors (official-format messages with latency, drops,
    20	             occlusion, bias), scenarios (seeded families), runner + monte_carlo (metrics, failure classes, seeds)
    21	```
    22	
    23	## Key decisions (and why)
    24	1. **Units/frames** mm/rad/s, y-up; conversion only at parser/publisher (geometry_model.md).
    25	2. **Two footprints**: convex envelope for everything we must not touch; true U-shape only for capture/physics.
    26	3. **Plan-per-leg**: a task replans from the observed cube after every leg; stale plans are never executed.
    27	4. **Spatial reservations** instead of time-indexed ones: execution timing is unreliable (motor lag, 50-300 ms vision
    28	   latency); a rover moves only inside its committed region, and committed regions are disjoint. WAIT is an action.
    29	   Deadlock -> the lower-priority / task-less rover is sent to a parking pose outside the other's intended region.
    30	5. **Two safety layers**: reservations (planning) + imminent-collision / board guard on commanded twists (reactive).
    31	6. **Estimation**: unicycle EKF (a diff-drive cannot slide sideways, so a [x,y,vx,vy] model has an unobservable
    32	   lateral mode); vision updates replayed at capture time; prediction never licenses blind driving (LOST => stop).
    33	7. **Freshness by capture time only** (Astra CRITICAL: the official vision can freeze with rising seq).
    34	8. **Cube orientation**: unknown until inferred from contact depth at capture (flush => heading mod 90) — a belief with
    35	   std, used as worst case over +-2 sigma for depot erosion.
    36	9. **Allocation**: exhaustive (<= 24 orderings x plan choices), makespan + robustness penalties, both rovers must work.
docs/geometry_model.md
     1	# Geometry model (canonical; all values live in `rover_strategy/config.py`)
     2	
     3	## Frames and units
     4	- Internal: mm, rad, s. `x = col * cell_mm`, `y = (rows - row) * cell_mm` (y UP), `theta = radians(theta_deg)` CCW from +x.
     5	  Official forward `(dcol, drow) = (cos, -sin)` maps to internal `(cos, sin)`; no sign flip of theta (verified by Astra
     6	  against `vision/detectors/rovers.py:176`). Example: official `(10, 20, 90 deg)` -> `(200, 460, pi/2)`.
     7	- Only `frames.py` / `vision/parser.py` / the simulator publisher convert units.
     8	- Angles wrapped to [-pi, pi). Cube orientation `alpha` is modulo 90 deg, stored in [-pi/4, pi/4).
     9	
    10	## Field
    11	Effective field 860 x 860 mm (43 x 43 cells of 20 mm, read from telemetry `grid`). Physical board 1000 x 1000,
    12	70 mm unused margin per side, no walls. Corner ArUco: 100 mm black + 20 mm white, centred on each field corner;
    13	its in-field quarter is a 70 x 70 mm square at each corner.
    14	
    15	## Rover (rover frame: origin = rotation centre, +x forward, +y left)
    16	| Quantity | Value | Status |
    17	|---|---|---|
    18	| Chassis length / outer width / channel width | 94.0 / 99.5 / 93.5 mm | DXF (separate pieces) + ruler (W_u = 94.05 +- 0.54) |
    19	| Paddle reach beyond front plate | 55 mm (rails 3 mm thick, 0-14 mm tall, touch the floor) | ruler |
    20	| Envelope | x in [-47, 102], y in [-49.75, 49.75] | derived; assumes axle at chassis centre |
    21	| Push face (front plate) | x = +47 | derived |
    22	| Rotation sweep radius | hypot(102, 49.75) = 113.49 mm | derived, conditional on axle position |
    23	| Wheel diameter / track | 33 / 89 mm | ruler |
    24	| Marker | 40 mm, ~90 mm high; residual offset to rotation centre unknown (vision config says 0) | provisional |
    25	
    26	Collision checks against anything the rover must not touch use the convex **envelope** (the channel counts as occupied),
    27	inflated by margins. The simulator and capture logic use the real U shape (`Footprint.parts`).
    28	
    29	## Cube
    30	60 mm square. Axis-aligned half extent for orientation `a`: `30(|cos a| + |sin a|)` in [30, 42.43].
    31	Unknown orientation => half diagonal 42.43 (disc obstacle).
    32	
    33	## Capture geometry (finding H-11, re-verified)
    34	Lateral capture tolerance `e_max(d) = (93.5 - 60(|cos d|+|sin d|)) / 2` in [4.32, 16.75] mm, `d` = push heading vs nearest face normal.
    35	With vision noise of the same order at 45 deg, capture of an unknown-orientation cube is not guaranteed; mitigations:
    36	stationary averaging before capture, orientation inference from contact geometry (below), verification + retry.
    37	
    38	**Orientation observability through contact:** when the cube rests against the front plate, the cube-centre distance
    39	from the plate equals its projected half-depth `30(|cos a|+|sin a|)`: 30 mm flush vs 42.4 mm at 45 deg. Measuring the
    40	cube centre in the rover frame during/after capture discriminates flush contact (orientation ~ push heading mod 90)
    41	from corner contact, up to sign. This is model-dependent (needs a calibrated marker/axle offset) and is treated as a
    42	belief with std, never as ground truth.
    43	
    44	## Distances
    45	- Pre-push distance (rotation centre to cube centre): `max(102, 113.49) + 42.43 + prepush_clearance + pose_uncertainty`
    46	  = 113.49 + 42.43 + 12 + 6 = **173.9 mm**. Sufficient (not necessary) for BOTH a straight approach without paddle
    47	  contact and a full in-place rotation at the pre-push pose for any cube orientation.
    48	- Contact distance (flush): 47 + 30 = 77 mm. Capture travel = 173.9 - 77 = ~97 mm.
    49	- Retreat before turning after a push: >= paddle reach + clearance so the paddles clear the cube (finding H-15).
    50	
    51	## Depot (ASSUMED, see rules_constraints.md)
    52	100 mm square centred on the published point. Valid cube-centre region for orientation `a` and margin `m`:
    53	the square eroded by the rotated cube: half-size `50 - 30(|cos a|+|sin a|) - m` -> +-14 mm aligned with m = 6;
    54	empty at 45 deg with m >= 7.6. Hence final legs into a depot should be axis-aligned.
docs/rules_constraints.md
     1	# Rules and constraints (authoritative interpretation after Astra Gate 1)
     2	
     3	Paths: `O/` = official repo (`_source/Rover Vision Artificial/Vision-Rover-Challenge-main_unz/Vision-Rover-Challenge-main/`).
     4	
     5	## Hard rules (official)
     6	| # | Rule | Source | How the software honours it |
     7	|---|---|---|---|
     8	| H1 | Software-only changes; no hardware/electronics changes; official vision may not be modified | `O/reglamento.md:13-24`, `O/robot.md:95` | We only consume telemetry v1; nothing depends on vision internals |
     9	| H2 | Fully autonomous after start: no manual commands, no touching robots/cubes, no code changes, no selective restarts | `O/reglamento.md:99-115` | Supervisor runs closed loop; all recovery is automatic |
    10	| H3 | Move only in phase `RUNNING`; stop immediately on `FINISHED` (and in IDLE/READY) | `O/vision-system/contrato/CONTRATO.md` s5 | Supervisor gates every command on phase; STOP otherwise |
    11	| H4 | Penalty for leaving the competition surface | `O/reglamento.md:152-158` | Whole rover envelope (incl. paddles) kept inside the 860x860 effective field minus `margins.board` (strict reading; `board.overhang_allowance_mm = 0`) |
    12	| H5 | Both robots must participate; success = both robots transport and deposit assigned objects | `O/reglamento.md:170-176`, `O/el_reto.md` success section | Allocator hard constraint `require_both_rovers` when both are healthy and >= 2 cubes remain |
    13	| H6 | Robots start from the established start zone | `O/reglamento.md:125` | Scenario family `start_zone`; planner itself assumes nothing about start poses |
    14	| H7 | Scoring: mainly time and number of correctly delivered objects; also placement precision, no collisions, recovery | `O/reglamento.md:176-180`, `O/el_reto.md` | Objective = makespan with robustness penalties; safety constraints are hard |
    15	| H8 | 2 or 3 cubes, unique colours; each goes to the depot of its colour; depot colours/positions read from telemetry | `CONTRATO.md` s3 | Everything keyed by colour/id; never by list index |
    16	| H9 | `obstacles` empty this edition but must be iterated | `CONTRATO.md` s3 | Parser converts them; planner treats them as obstacles if ever present |
    17	
    18	## Telemetry semantics that constrain the design
    19	- TCP NDJSON port 2026, ~20 Hz, last-value-wins (seq gaps normal). `ts_ms` = CAPTURE time. `age_ms` = time since last real observation of that object.
    20	- **Freeze hazard (Astra CRITICAL):** if two corner markers are occluded the official vision keeps publishing with rising `seq` but frozen `ts_ms` / ages. Freshness is therefore judged ONLY from capture time (`ts_ms - age_ms`), duplicate capture times are ignored, and rovers stop when data is stale (`TelemetryPolicy`).
    21	- Cube orientation is NOT published (v1). Cube position = centre of the fitted base square; unreliable detections do not refresh (last position kept, age grows). With ~70 % occlusion fitting errors reach ~34 mm, so stale/occluded cube positions get growing uncertainty.
    22	- Published rover pose = marker pose corrected by the vision's configured marker->rotation-centre offsets (currently 0, position offset NOT measured) and marker-height parallax. `config.rover.marker_offset_*` is only a residual correction (default 0) to avoid double compensation.
    23	
    24	## Unknowns and the defaults chosen (each is a config knob)
    25	| Unknown | Default | Risk | Knob |
    26	|---|---|---|---|
    27	| Depot size/shape and delivery criterion (no official definition; positions marked PROVISIONAL) | 100 mm square centred on the published point; delivered = whole cube footprint inside | Could be larger (easier) or a point tolerance | `depot.half_size`, `depot.delivery_margin` |
    28	| Corner marker vs depot overlap | Depot point is the inner corner of the 100 mm corner marker; a delivered cube overlaps the marker (>= 10x10 mm black even at the best spot). Two occluded markers freeze vision. | Could make the 3rd delivery impossible on the current provisional layout | delivery-point cost; flagged in plan notes |
    29	| Rover rotation centre vs chassis centre | coincident | footprint error of a few mm | `rover.chassis_center_x` + `margins.pose_uncertainty` |
    30	| Throttle -> mm/s calibration | 180 mm/s at throttle 1 (sim) | speeds/timeouts off | `limits.*`; must be measured per wheel, fwd/rev, under load |
    31	| Start zone size / arrangement | official sim poses (4,4) and (4,8) cells, 45 deg | none for the planner | scenario family |
    32	| Round time limit | none known | — | sim run limit 300 s |
    33	
    34	## Firmware conventions (for later embedded work)
    35	- `motor_1` = left, `motor_2` = right; throttle in [-1, 1], positive = forward (`O/codigos/test_motores.py`).
    36	- `O/codigos/turn_angle.py` treats POSITIVE angles as CLOCKWISE — opposite to telemetry/our CCW convention. Our command interface is wheel speeds with omega = (v_right - v_left) / track, never that helper's angle sign.
    37	- A motor-command watchdog on the rover (stop if no fresh command within ~300 ms) is required for real hardware.

